1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,910
<font color="#000000">Modern matrices can hold billions of entries, yet
they're often nearly low rank.</font>

2
00:00:05,990 --> 00:00:08,312
<font color="#000000">This Stanford work introduces LPLR,</font>

3
00:00:08,392 --> 00:00:13,528
<font color="#000000">a randomized algorithm that factorizes any matrix
into two low-rank factors,</font>

4
00:00:13,608 --> 00:00:16,067
<font color="#000000">then quantizes them to low precision,</font>

5
00:00:16,147 --> 00:00:21,625
<font color="#000000">compressing to one bit per coordinate while
matching prior compression on images,</font>

6
00:00:21,705 --> 00:00:23,890
<font color="#000000">embeddings, and LlaMa-7b weights.</font>

7
00:00:24,270 --> 00:00:27,902
<font color="#000000">Matrices are everywhere in science and machine learning,</font>

8
00:00:27,982 --> 00:00:32,741
<font color="#000000">but modern ones hold billions of elements that
strain memory and compute.</font>

9
00:00:32,821 --> 00:00:35,458
<font color="#000000">Luckily, many are approximately low rank.</font>

10
00:00:35,538 --> 00:00:40,960
<font color="#000000">Prior work exploited either low-rank structure or
low-precision quantization alone;</font>

11
00:00:41,040 --> 00:00:46,130
<font color="#000000">combining both in one factorization, with provable
error control, stayed open.</font>

12
00:00:46,510 --> 00:00:51,705
<font color="#000000">If a matrix is low rank, we can write it as a tall
factor times a wide one,</font>

13
00:00:51,785 --> 00:00:54,940
<font color="#000000">and storing those in low precision saves more.</font>

14
00:00:55,020 --> 00:00:59,512
<font color="#000000">But doing this crudely at low bit budgets degrades
accuracy fast,</font>

15
00:00:59,592 --> 00:01:04,435
<font color="#000000">and the exact SVD route costs order n d squared,
prohibitive at scale.</font>

16
00:01:04,515 --> 00:01:09,710
<font color="#000000">It seeks a randomized way to get both factors with
small, analyzable error.</font>

17
00:01:10,090 --> 00:01:12,138
<font color="#000000">The core contribution is LPLR,</font>

18
00:01:12,218 --> 00:01:18,735
<font color="#000000">Low-Precision Low-Rank factorization: a randomized
algorithm that exploits low-rank structure</font>

19
00:01:18,815 --> 00:01:21,289
<font color="#000000">and quantizes the resulting factors.</font>

20
00:01:21,369 --> 00:01:28,880
<font color="#000000">The authors derive rigorous upper bounds on the
approximation error in terms of target rank and bit budget,</font>

21
00:01:28,960 --> 00:01:32,356
<font color="#000000">exposing a tunable compression-accuracy tradeoff.</font>

22
00:01:32,436 --> 00:01:36,896
<font color="#000000">They add an SVD-based variant, LPLR-SVD, and
validate on images,</font>

23
00:01:36,976 --> 00:01:40,230
<font color="#000000">embedding classification, and LlaMa-7b weights.</font>

24
00:01:40,610 --> 00:01:42,315
<font color="#FFFFFF">LPLR runs in a few steps.</font>

25
00:01:42,395 --> 00:01:47,312
<font color="#FFFFFF">It draws a Gaussian sketching matrix S and forms
the sketch A times S,</font>

26
00:01:47,392 --> 00:01:50,952
<font color="#FFFFFF">a randomized rangefinder for the column space of A,</font>

27
00:01:51,032 --> 00:01:53,380
<font color="#FFFFFF">then quantizes it to get Q of A S.</font>

28
00:01:53,460 --> 00:01:58,305
<font color="#FFFFFF">Next it least-squares-projects A's columns onto
this quantized basis,</font>

29
00:01:58,385 --> 00:02:02,660
<font color="#FFFFFF">solving for the projection W star, then quantizes W
star too,</font>

30
00:02:02,740 --> 00:02:04,516
<font color="#FFFFFF">returning factors L and R.</font>

31
00:02:04,596 --> 00:02:11,797
<font color="#FFFFFF">The key insight: Gaussian sketches are
Johnson-Lindenstrauss embeddings with an equalization property,</font>

32
00:02:11,877 --> 00:02:16,937
<font color="#FFFFFF">so reconstruction error stays order one and doesn't
grow with dimension,</font>

33
00:02:17,017 --> 00:02:19,650
<font color="#FFFFFF">keeping LPLR accurate even at one bit.</font>

34
00:02:20,030 --> 00:02:24,184
<font color="#000000">Experiments span a deliberately diverse set of matrices.</font>

35
00:02:24,264 --> 00:02:30,612
<font color="#000000">For images: the Shepp-Logan phantom, a Hubble image
of Jupiter, and an MR brain scan.</font>

36
00:02:30,692 --> 00:02:34,846
<font color="#000000">For embeddings: CIFAR-10 and CIFAR-100 from MobileNetV3,</font>

37
00:02:34,926 --> 00:02:41,122
<font color="#000000">and IMDB and Emotion text from BERT, scored by a
three-nearest-neighbor classifier.</font>

38
00:02:41,202 --> 00:02:44,601
<font color="#000000">And for LLMs, the weight matrices of LlaMa-7b.</font>

39
00:02:44,681 --> 00:02:50,650
<font color="#000000">Every method gets the same total bit budget,
reporting relative Frobenius error.</font>

40
00:02:51,030 --> 00:02:56,552
<font color="#000000">The headline: LPLR delivers extreme compression,
near one bit per coordinate,</font>

41
00:02:56,632 --> 00:02:58,662
<font color="#000000">while preserving performance.</font>

42
00:02:58,742 --> 00:03:04,410
<font color="#000000">On CIFAR-10 embeddings at a single bit, it retains
ninety-two percent accuracy,</font>

43
00:03:04,490 --> 00:03:10,376
<font color="#000000">matching the unquantized ninety-one, while naive
quantization collapses to eleven.</font>

44
00:03:10,456 --> 00:03:14,159
<font color="#000000">On CIFAR-100, seventy-nine percent versus about one.</font>

45
00:03:14,239 --> 00:03:15,614
<font color="#000000">On LlaMa-7b weights,</font>

46
00:03:15,694 --> 00:03:20,925
<font color="#000000">LPLR-SVD reaches mean relative Frobenius error near
zero point five four,</font>

47
00:03:21,005 --> 00:03:25,800
<font color="#000000">against zero point eight four for naive quant, with
lower variance.</font>

48
00:03:26,180 --> 00:03:31,302
<font color="#000000">The paper separates two effects: low-rank structure
and quantization precision.</font>

49
00:03:31,382 --> 00:03:35,188
<font color="#000000">Table 3 sweeps the bit-budget triplet at fixed
compression,</font>

50
00:03:35,268 --> 00:03:41,049
<font color="#000000">showing that shifting budget toward the
non-quantized reference steadily lowers Frobenius</font>

51
00:03:41,129 --> 00:03:43,353
<font color="#000000">error for LPLR and its SVD variant.</font>

52
00:03:43,433 --> 00:03:44,934
<font color="#000000">Across embedding tables,</font>

53
00:03:45,014 --> 00:03:51,651
<font color="#000000">raising the quantization budget from one to four
bits moves LPLR from beating baselines toward parity,</font>

54
00:03:51,731 --> 00:03:54,680
<font color="#000000">its edge largest where compression is extreme.</font>

55
00:03:55,060 --> 00:03:59,344
<font color="#000000">By the numbers: LPLR compresses to one bit per coordinate.</font>

56
00:03:59,424 --> 00:04:03,709
<font color="#000000">On CIFAR-10 at one bit, ninety-two percent accuracy
versus eleven for naive quant,</font>

57
00:04:03,789 --> 00:04:08,073
<font color="#000000">matching the unquantized ninety-one.</font>

58
00:04:08,153 --> 00:04:12,437
<font color="#000000">On CIFAR-100, seventy-nine versus one.</font>

59
00:04:12,517 --> 00:04:16,801
<font color="#000000">On LlaMa-7b,</font>

60
00:04:16,881 --> 00:04:21,166
<font color="#000000">mean Frobenius error zero point five four for
LPLR-SVD versus zero point eight four.</font>

61
00:04:21,246 --> 00:04:25,530
<font color="#000000">And it runs in order n d m time, far cheaper than a
full SVD.</font>

62
00:04:25,910 --> 00:04:29,978
<font color="#000000">The takeaway: low rank and low precision are
complementary,</font>

63
00:04:30,058 --> 00:04:32,649
<font color="#000000">and exploiting them together pays off.</font>

64
00:04:32,729 --> 00:04:37,992
<font color="#000000">By sketching a matrix with a Gaussian projection
and quantizing the factors,</font>

65
00:04:38,072 --> 00:04:43,476
<font color="#000000">LPLR compresses to one bit per coordinate while
preserving accuracy on images,</font>

66
00:04:43,556 --> 00:04:47,413
<font color="#000000">embeddings, and LLM weights, with provable error bounds.</font>

67
00:04:47,493 --> 00:04:51,490
<font color="#000000">And by avoiding costly SVD, it scales to largest matrices.</font>
